  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered storms have ended in the SE part of the \nregion, no additional activity is expected tonight. \n\nThe showers/storms over SE NC have pushed offshore. The cold front \nhad pushed through Fayetteville and will push just to the SE of \nClinton and Goldsboro in the next few hours. Regardless, much drier \nair upstream over the Piedmont will begin to advect in from the \nnorth over SE sections in the next few hours and no additional storm \ndevelopment is expected. \n\nIt will otherwise be mild for late August, and mainly clear \novernight. low temperatures 50s in rural Piedmont locations to \nlower to mid 60s SE. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Diurnally driven storms expected to return from mid \nweek into the weekend, with the greatest chances on Thursday and \nFriday. \n\nA longwave low amplitude H5 trough over the Ohio Valley will \ngradually slide eastward by the middle of the week, as the influence \nof offshore ridging gradually wanes across the region. This will \nallow for a gradual increase in moisture as weak southerly flow \nimports anomalously high PW's into the area as early as \nWednesday/Thursday. Meanwhile, the longwave trough and attendant \nsurface cold front will approach the area from the northwest on \nThursday, serving as a focal point for shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment area-wide. It would appear the greatest storm chances on \nThursday would be across the western Piedmont in closest proximity \nto the front and weak Piedmont trough, although pretty much anywhere \nacross central NC will have increased storm chances given abundant \nPW's and MLCAPEs around 1000-1500 J/kG. \n\nAdditional shower and thunderstorm chances are expected area-wide on \nFriday as the surface front slides into NC.  How far southward this \nfront makes it before it stalls out is likely the biggest piece of \nforecast uncertainty that we're encountering today. The lack of \nstrong post-frontal northwesterly flow aloft would suggest the front \nwill likely stall over NC, then buckle back northward or wash out \ncompletely this weekend. Naturally this would allow for a continued \nperiod of afternoon shower and thunderstorm chances. With the flow \naloft gradually taking on a more zonal component this weekend, \nconvection within the Piedmont trough as well as sea breeze induced \nstorms seem like the most likely outcome.",
